<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles VI of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1368–1422</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles the Mad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles VI, called the Beloved and later the Mad, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1380 to 1422. His recurring bouts of insanity from 1392 left power to rival princes and led to civil war between the Armagnac and Burgundian factions. Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> exploited the conflict, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> in 1415, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> in 1420 Charles disinherited his son in favour of Henry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france.jpg" alt="Charles VI of France" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Mazarine Master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_VI_de_France_-_Dialogues_de_Pierre_Salmon_-_Bib_de_Gen%C3%A8ve_MsFr165f4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
